{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for founding revision 2 at a5185fa671f1b373a64f695e6e5450ea7257ca36, resting on all 39 artifacts with packages/room/README.md first.\n\n[P1] An unanswered retirement delete can remove the newly founded canonical repository. In packages/git/src/workspace/workspaces.ts, retireCanonical marks earlier repo-delete/in-flight duties superseded when a later delete answers. prepareCanonical then recreates the same repository name and sealCanonical accepts it; the pending earlier delete is no longer fenced. A second answered delete is not proof that the first request completed or was cancelled.\n\nAn independent Node/SQLite control uses the candidate's Workspaces and existing fake namespace, with the same delayed-effect pattern as its late-create test: an unheld repository exists; the first delete throws a transport TypeError before its retained effect applies; a restarted retry's delete answers, recreates canon, pushes the first commit, revokes its token, and seals. Every duty is done and canonicalDue is null. Applying the first delete afterward removes canon and main. reconcile returns zero and no recovery is scheduled. The healthy control passes. This can erase a repository after the Room has begun ordinary work.\n\nDo not retire an unresolved destructive operation merely because a retry answered or a current read shows absence. Fence deletes to a stable repository incarnation with conditional deletion, obtain provider completion/cancellation proof for every earlier attempt, or retain durable blocking uncertainty and avoid reusing the name until it is safe. Keep alarm/administrative visibility for unresolved deletes, and make the seal check include unresolved destructive effects. Add the late-delete case across restart, then repeat after a successful seal and a normal landing; neither the live canonical repository nor its later contents may disappear. Cover a lost delete followed by NOT_FOUND too. If the provider offers a stronger